ant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;0gZwO8N6fM2zMgW36SxfphTaO73mhix8HRauk8XpMCoƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;WPc6celfqZ-zHrmMX9xr4bbKdpgp1fbkQEwPfl8s7CAƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkd3.98ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Fa4ybNJ6xcwaYNma8fz-LizxaRgBwc4DC2PdwIQ-NKIƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkkenvironmentƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;PskSDR9oyuytB34JDYioj00ygNYvI8DlyRVNnWxWruoƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkd5091ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;W_wW2zVOspN552vq-BW8kP_rKeiXvOeaLGr79GdcQJsƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx2026-07-18T04:08:15.342Zƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;_thRddk7LePgKu58lgONRw5xS9Rntav7sEARcgR_WR8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxkoios.source-driven-nominationƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;LZHSubOQS-B6eMZpvz_ETHO06ELhpmER2ihiEXs9hVcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarke21.02ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;bQ23WJsIb07xTXXWtcZ1DduptxUg_bMAssujaomMRT0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx2026-07-17T15:55:16.000Zƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;SGtOadCfGtWl2HhkzTK9iWCeSZhjuG_qDUgzRv4H-Sgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxKwildfire-smoke-threatens-the-world-cup-final-fifa-still-doesn-t-have-a-planƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;DRulMM8UJ8T9iIJUx-ghTcUFn9JdIyWJz_Cqxmu6mTUƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkyhThe biggest game in soccer is set for Sunday, when Argentina and Spain will meet in the World Cup final, held just outside of New York City. Thereâ€™s just one problem â€” wildfire smoke now threatens the match and FIFA, the sportâ€™s governing body, doesnâ€™t appear to have any plan in place to protect player or fan health.&nbsp; Wildfire smoke blowing from Canada has blanketed large swaths of the Midwest and East Coast in recent days, i mpacting more than a dozen states , with some of the worst-hit cities facing air quality in ranges considered hazardous. New Yorkers are among those who have been watching their air turn orange and hazy this week. On Thursday, the state issued an alert as the air quality index climbed to â€œunhealthyâ€ and â€œ very unhealthyâ€ levels. â€œAvoid spending time outdoors, if possible,â€ officials advised. As of Friday morning, air quality near the stadium remained in the unhealthy range, and while itâ€™s forecast to improve to â€œmoderateâ€ by Sundayâ€™s kickoff, smoke patterns could still change. If concerns continue during the finals, thereâ€™s no way to close off the open-air stadium where stars Lionel Messi and Lamine Yamal are slated to square off. That means fans and players would almost certainly be exposed to smoke, which could lead to symptoms such as a burning throat, coughing, or headache, among others. This can be particularly dangerous for sensitive groups, including children, older individuals, and people with respiratory conditions such as asthma. Players have also practiced outdoors during air quality events in the lead up to the game â€” the Spanish team held a practice Thursday in New Jersey. â€œThese are high-level athletes who are moving a lot of air through their lungs,â€ Dr. Courtney Howard, an emergency room physician and Global Climate and Health Alliance official, told the Associated Press . â€œThey shouldnâ€™t be practicing outside if the air quality levels are at hazardous sort of ranges for wildfire-related air pollution.â€ World Cup organizer FIFA does not appear to have any contingency plans in place for wildfire smoke. Neither FIFA nor New York City Department of Health officials immediately responded to Gristâ€™s request for comment. In a previous statement to Grist , FIFA detailed extensive protocols related to extreme heat, including mandatory water breaks for players, but didnâ€™t mention air quality.&nbsp; â€œClimate-related risks,â€ the organization wrote last month, â€œare assessed as part of overall tournament planning and managed in close coordination with host cities, stadium authorities, and national agencies.â€&nbsp; Unlike FIFA, a number of other leagues implement standardized adjustments triggered by air quality warnings. Per National Womenâ€™s Soccer League policy, for instance, a womenâ€™s soccer game at Citi Field in New York on Thursday had additional hydration breaks for safety. NWSL policy adds hydration breaks at an air quality index of 101 (â€œunhealthy for sensitive groupsâ€) and cancels or postpones games when the AQI tops 200 (â€œvery unhealthyâ€). Major League Baseball rescheduled one game this week in Philadelphia, and Major League Soccer canceled a game in Chicago due to smoke. The World Cup is in its fifth and final week, and despite the games taking place across multiple wildfire-prone regions, so far FIFAâ€™s bet that the air would stay clear has paid off. It may get lucky again this weekend, says Nicholas Watanabe, a professor of sport and entertainment management at the University of South Carolina, but that doesnâ€™t excuse not being ready. â€œFIFA seems unprepared for these wildfires,â€ said Watanabe. â€œAt the same time, it looks like the worst of the pollution will dissipate before the World Cup Final, so FIFA will dodge a bullet and likely push forward with the match.â€ Some, however, question whether enough is being done, both by FIFA and other outdoor sports leagues facing these and other climate threats. FIFA has already been criticized this year for its policies regarding extreme heat .â€If we have to have a hydration break every 15 minutes, then we shouldnâ€™t be playing the game,â€ said Trinity Rodman , who played in the smoke-affected NWSL soccer game in New York.&nbsp; One professional cyclist recently questioned whether the Tour de France should be held in the summer, as this yearâ€™s iteration of the iconic event has been plagued by wildfires, smoke, and extreme heat. â€œI would change the whole calendar,â€ said yellow jersey holder Tadej PogaÄar earlier this month. â€œItâ€™s not something I can do.â€ The power to make changes generally rests with a sportâ€™s governing body, rather than athletes, and Watanabe thinks climate issues should be taken more seriously. Even if the World Cup final is spared any impacts, he said this close call should serve as a warning. â€œHopefully,â€ he said, â€œthis is a wakeup call to FIFA and all other sport leagues and competitions of the need for regulations and a comprehensive plan to deal with climate risks.â€ This story was originally published by Grist with the headline Wildfire smoke threatens the World Cup final. FIFA still doesnâ€™t have a plan. on Jul 17, 2026.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;XGwyXGKMlGCelNsfKAdu-h2SC9tJcF8jh14w-CAvlXIƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx2wildfire smoke threatens cup final fifa doesn planƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;bdprMkQYqOcCsIZg3RBb-0L8KzZXE4NAfcD8qO08Y4Mƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxOWildfire smoke threatens the World Cup final. FIFA still doesnâ€™t have a plan.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;JntTfQ86LzX1py0ftBxaf8i6CbReeL_b4WcCzoDcURYƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdun